# Copy this file to `.env` in the same folder and fill in your own values.
#   Supabase dashboard -> your project -> Project Settings -> API
#
# NEVER put the service_role / sb_secret_ key here. This file is bundled into
# the app, so anyone can read it. A secret key bypasses every RLS policy.
#
# The public key has two possible names; either works (see src/lib/supabase.ts):
#   publishable  ->  sb_publishable_...   (newer projects)
#   anon         ->  eyJhbGciOi...        (older projects)
# This file uses the publishable name; EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY and
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_KEY are also accepted.

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=YOUR-PUBLIC-KEY

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development-only demo sign-in (optional — delete this block and it vanishes)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app signs in with a phone number and a one-time code, which needs an SMS
# provider. Until one is configured there are no accounts, so nothing behind
# sign-in can be looked at. `supabase/demo-accounts.sql` creates a customer, a
# rider and a shop owner instead, and these lines are what make them appear as
# buttons on the login screen.
#
# Leave an address out and its button is simply not shown. The whole block is
# behind __DEV__, so it never appears in a real build even if the lines remain.
# The password is the one you passed to demo-accounts.sql with -v demo_password.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_DEMO_PASSWORD=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_DEMO_CUSTOMER_EMAIL=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_DEMO_DELIVERY_EMAIL=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_DEMO_ADMIN_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Store-review access (optional — only for the build submitted to the stores)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apple's App Review guideline 2.1(a) expects a working demo login, and Google
# Play asks for working credentials too. `__DEV__` cannot help here: the reviewer
# installs a *release* build. So the login screen keeps one extra door that is
# NOT tied to __DEV__ — it opens only when both lines below are set, which they
# are only in the `review` EAS profile.
#
# This is ONE account with the CUSTOMER role. Never point it at an admin: the
# reviewer only needs to browse, buy and see their own orders.
#
# It must be turned OFF for the live production build once the app is approved:
# a self-serve published login into real customer data is not something to leave
# lying around. Prefer an account whose seed data is demo data only.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_REVIEW_EMAIL=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_REVIEW_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Crash reporting (optional — no DSN means no reporting, and no noise)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sentry catches the errors this app throws on a phone in Kunigal that the
# developer's laptop never sees. Leave this blank and nothing is reported.
#
# Read this before worrying that a key is in the app: a Sentry DSN is a
# WRITE-ONLY ingest key. It can send events to one project and cannot read
# anything back. It is designed to ship in a client binary, and it is NOT the
# same thing as a Supabase secret key (see the warning at the top of this file).
# It is still not committed here — `.env` is git-ignored, and this line is a
# template.
#
# What it does NOT send: the SDK is started with `sendDefaultPii: false` and a
# `beforeSend` that strips the user block, cookies and query strings, so no
# customer name, phone number or delivery address leaves the phone. See
# `src/lib/crash-reporting.ts`.
#
# Native crashes (the app dying in Kotlin/Swift) are only captured in a real
# development or release build — NOT in Expo Go. See PRODUCTION.md.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://YOUR-KEY@o000000.ingest.sentry.io/0000000